{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017NRNcuBtuQsg1dz4wXXN6e","type":"tool_result","content":"40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendry\n42:Prior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability\n66:\\paragraph{Benchmark suites and their overlap.} Multi-task suites aggregate many sub-benchmarks \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB}. Agreement analyses across benchmarks \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench} bear on how related benchmark scores are; here the\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}\n70:\\paragraph{Latent-capability and scaling views.} Observational scaling laws and skill scaling laws explain multi-benchmark performance through a few latent capability factors estimated from public evaluations \\citep{Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}, and item response theory has long mo\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2\n88:If benchmark scores are jointly Gaussian with covariance $\\Sigma$ and benchmarks in $S$ are observed, the conditional mean $\\mathbb{E}[y_U\\mid y_S]$ ($U$ the unrun benchmarks) minimises mean squared error, and the expected squared error summed over $U$ equals $\\operatorname{tr}\\Sigma_{U\\mid S}$. \n91:(3) a per-target non-negative-least-squares stack \\citep{Lawson1995NNLS,Breiman1996Stacked} lets each target benchmark weight several imputers (Gaussian conditional means, ridge, nearest-neighbour models, column median), with a median-shrinkage fallback when the stack does not beat the median in \n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{H\n201:\\paragraph{Scope.} Three leaderboards, of which two contain generative language models in small numbers of benchmarks (six and ten) and one contains text-embedding models; metric types differ (accuracy-type, F1, exact-match and BLEU-type metrics). Family-disjoint splitting was defined by name an","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44b78197-b41d-462e-9460-15eef4b09990","uuid":"e304e480-96b4-4826-88ea-b651fa3ea3b6","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:18:51.760Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendry\n42:Prior work on cheaper evaluation mostly reduces the number of \\emph{items} within one benchmark \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}, distills a handful of benchmarks into a sparse one \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}, or fits low-dimensional capability\n